= i
{parent=2007/d/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/d/3/i}

A <coordinate transformation> is a continuously differentiable <bijection> from the <open set> $U$ onto an open subset $T(U)\subset\mathbb R^2$, with continuously differentiable inverse. Equivalently, it is a $C^1$ <diffeomorphism>; its <Jacobian determinant> never vanishes. The <inverse function theorem> supplies the local inverse, while global <injectivity> is an additional requirement.

= ii
{parent=2007/d/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/d/3/ii}

The <change of variables formula> for a <Lebesgue integrable function> is
$$
\boxed{\int_{T(U)}f(u,v)\,du\,dv
=\int_U f(T(x,y))\,|\det DT(x,y)|\,dx\,dy.}
$$
The absolute <Jacobian determinant> accounts for area regardless of orientation. The formula also holds for nonnegative measurable functions with possibly infinite <integral>.

= iii
{parent=2007/d/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/d/3/iii}

Put $u=x-y$ and $v=x+y$. The square becomes $(-1,1)^2$, with $x=(u+v)/2$, $y=(v-u)/2$ and absolute <Jacobian determinant> $1/2$. On its support the denominator $v+2$ exceeds one and $|u|<1$, so the nonnegative integrand is bounded by one on a set of finite area; it is therefore <Lebesgue integrable>. The <change of variables formula> and <Fubini's theorem> give
$$
\int_{\mathbb R^2}f^{2n}
=\frac12\int_{-1}^1u^{2n}\,du\int_{-1}^1(v+2)^{-2n}\,dv
=\boxed{\frac{1-3^{1-2n}}{(2n-1)(2n+1)}}.
$$

= 4
{parent=2007/d}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2007/d/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/d/4/i}

The <dominated convergence theorem> states: if measurable $f_n$ converge almost everywhere to $f$ and $|f_n|\le g$ almost everywhere for an integrable $g\ge0$, then $f$ is integrable and $\int f_n\to\int f$. In fact $\int|f_n-f|\to0$.

To derive it from the <monotone convergence theorem>, first prove <Fatou's lemma>. For nonnegative measurable $h_n$, the functions $k_N=\inf_{n\ge N}h_n$ increase to $\liminf h_n$ and satisfy $\int k_N\le\inf_{n\ge N}\int h_n$. <Monotone convergence> yields $\int\liminf h_n\le\liminf\int h_n$.

Now $|f|\le g$ almost everywhere, so $f$ is integrable. Apply <Fatou's lemma> to the nonnegative functions $2g-|f_n-f|$, which converge almost everywhere to $2g$:
$$
2\int g\le2\int g-\limsup_n\int|f_n-f|.
$$
Hence $\int|f_n-f|\to0$, and $|\int f_n-\int f|\le\int|f_n-f|$ proves the assertion. Common exceptional null sets may be removed at the beginning since the sequence is countable.

= ii
{parent=2007/d/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/d/4/ii}

For fixed $x>0$, $f_n(x)\to e^{-x}\sin x$; for $x\le0$, the limit is zero. On $0<x<n$, the supplied inequality gives $(1-x/n)^n\le e^{-x}$, so
$$
|f_n(x)|\le\chi_{(0,\infty)}(x)e^{-x}.
$$
This is the given integrable dominating function. By the <dominated convergence theorem>,
$$
\lim_n\int f_n(x)\,dx=\int_0^\infty e^{-x}\sin x\,dx
=\operatorname{Im}\frac1{1-i}=\boxed{\frac12}.
$$

= E
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 3
{parent=2007/e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/3}

A <topological space> is <compact> if every open cover has a finite subcover. It is <connected> if it cannot be written as the union of two disjoint nonempty open subsets. It is <Hausdorff> if every two distinct points have disjoint open neighborhoods. These definitions apply to the space itself and, with the <subspace topology>, to its subsets.

= a
{parent=2007/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/3/a}

**True.** If open sets cover $Y$, their <inverse images> under the continuous <surjection> $f$ cover $X$. <Compactness> of $X$ gives a finite subcover; <surjectivity> then makes the corresponding finite family cover $Y$. This proves the <compactness> of a <continuous image of a compact space>.

= b
{parent=2007/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/3/b}

**True.** A separation of $Y$ into two disjoint nonempty open subsets would pull back under the continuous <surjection> $f$ to a separation of $X$. The <inverse images> are nonempty by <surjectivity>, contradicting <connectedness> of $X$. Thus a <continuous image of a connected space> is connected.

= c
{parent=2007/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/3/c}

**True.** The <Hausdorff> assumption makes $\{y\}$ closed in $Y$. Its <inverse image> under the <continuous map> $f$ is closed in the <compact space> $X$, and a <closed subset of a compact space> is compact. This is the <compactness> of the fiber with its <subspace topology>.

= d
{parent=2007/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/3/d}

**False.** Take $X=[0,1]$, $Y=S^1$ and the <surjection> $f(t)=e^{2\pi it}$. The interval is <compact> and <connected>, and the circle is <Hausdorff>. But $f^{-1}(1)=\{0,1\}$ is a disconnected two-point subspace. Thus a continuous <surjection> need not have connected fibers.

= e
{parent=2007/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/3/e}

**False.** Let $X$ be a two-point set with the <indiscrete topology>, let $Y$ be a one-point space, and let $f$ be the unique map. The space $X$ is finite and hence <compact>; its only nonempty open subset is all of $X$, so it is <connected>. It is not <Hausdorff>, whereas $Y$ is <Hausdorff> and $f$ is a continuous <surjection>.

= 4
{parent=2007/e}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2007/e/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/4/a}

For the canonical <surjection> $p:X\to X/{\sim}$, define a subset $W$ of $X/{\sim}$ to be open exactly when $p^{-1}(W)$ is open in $X$. This is the <quotient topology>. The empty set and the whole quotient have open <inverse images>. Moreover <inverse images> commute with arbitrary unions and finite intersections, so the corresponding axioms of a <topology> follow directly from those on $X$.

The map $p$ need not be an <open map>. For example, on $X=\mathbb R$ identify every point of $[0,1]$ to one point and leave all other classes singletons. The set $U=(-1/2,1/2)$ is open, but
$$
p^{-1}(p(U))=(-1/2,1]
$$
is not open. The definition of the <quotient topology> therefore shows that $p(U)$ is not open. The obstruction is that saturating an <open set> by <equivalence classes> need not preserve openness.

= b
{parent=2007/e/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/e/4/b}

Treat the two squares as a single edge-pairing construction, including the identifications between different squares. Number the corners of the left square counterclockwise from its lower left corner by $A,B,C,D$, and similarly use $E,F,G,H$ on the right. The single arrows identify $D$ with $H$ and $C$ with $E$. The double arrows identify $G$ with $F$ and $H$ with $G$. The triple arrows identify $A$ with $E$ and $B$ with $F$. The quadruple arrows identify $A$ with $C$ and $D$ with $B$.

Thus there are precisely two <vertex> classes, $\{A,C,E\}$ and $\{B,D,F,G,H\}$. There are four <edge> classes and two two-dimensional cells, so the resulting closed connected <surface> has <Euler characteristic>
$$
\chi=2-4+2=0.
$$
It is a <nonorientable surface>: on the left square the quadruple-arrow sides have the same orientation relative to a traversal of its boundary, giving a twisted band after gluing. Equivalently, no choice of orientations for the two faces makes the induced directions opposite on every paired <edge>. The <vertex> links are circles, so these <edge> identifications produce a closed <surface>, rather than a singular cell space. The <classification of compact surfaces> now gives
$$
\boxed{\text{Klein bottle}.}
$$
A <torus> also has <Euler characteristic> zero, but is excluded by the failed orientation test.

= F
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 2
{parent=2007/f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/f/2}

Differentiability at $a$ means that there is a <linear map> $L:V\to V_1$ with
$$
\frac{\|f(a+h)-f(a)-Lh\|_1}{\|h\|}\longrightarrow0\quad(h\to0).
$$
This map is the <total derivative> $Df_a$. In the <Frobenius norm>, $|AB|\le|A||B|$, by the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> applied to <matrix> entries. Expanding with the order of all factors preserved gives
$$
(A+H)^3=A^3+A^2H+AHA+HA^2+AH^2+HAH+H^2A+H^3.
$$
The remainder after the terms linear in $H$ has norm at most $3|A||H|^2+|H|^3=o(|H|)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{Df_A(H)=A^2H+AHA+HA^2.}
$$
For $|C|<1$, the <Neumann series> $I-C+C^2-\cdots$ converges absolutely in the finite-dimensional space of <matrices>. Multiplying its partial sums by $I+C$ and passing to the limit shows that it is $(I+C)^{-1}$. Its remainder after $I-C$ has norm at most $|C|^2/(1-|C|)=o(|C|)$, proving the required expansion.

If $A$ is an <invertible matrix> and $|H|<1/|A^{-1}|$, then $A+H=A(I+A^{-1}H)$ is an <invertible matrix>. Thus the <general linear group> is an open subset of the <matrix> space. The preceding expansion also gives
$$
(A+H)^{-1}=A^{-1}-A^{-1}HA^{-1}+o(|H|).
$$
Apply the <chain rule> to $g(X)=(X^{-1})^3$, using the cubic <total derivative> at $A^{-1}$ and this <total derivative> of inversion:
$$
\boxed{Dg_A(H)=-A^{-3}HA^{-1}-A^{-2}HA^{-2}-A^{-1}HA^{-3}.}
$$
The three terms generally cannot be combined by commuting $A$ and $H$.

= G
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 2
{parent=2007/g}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2007/g/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/g/2/i}

Parametrize the <surface of revolution> by
$$
\mathbf r(x,\theta)=(x,y(x)\cos\theta,y(x)\sin\theta),\qquad0\le\theta\le2\pi.
$$
The magnitude of the <cross product> $\mathbf r_x\times\mathbf r_\theta$ is $y\sqrt{1+y'^2}$. Integrating the <surface area element> over $\theta$ gives
$$
\boxed{J[y]=2\pi\int_{x_1}^{x_2}y(x)\sqrt{1+y'(x)^2}\,dx.}
$$
Positivity of $y$ removes an otherwise necessary absolute value in the area element.

= ii
{parent=2007/g/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/g/2/ii}

For $F(y,y')=y\sqrt{1+y'^2}$, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\sqrt{1+y'^2}-\frac d{dx}\frac{yy'}{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}=0,
\qquad yy''=1+y'^2.
$$
Since $F$ has no explicit $x$ dependence, the <Beltrami identity> gives
$$
F-y'F_{y'}=\frac{y}{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}=C>0.
$$
Solving this <first integral>, or substituting into the second-order equation, yields
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C\cosh\!\left(\frac{x-K}{C}\right),\qquad C>0.}
$$
The constants satisfy $y_1=C\cosh((x_1-K)/C)$ and $y_2=C\cosh((x_2-K)/C)$. There need not be a solution for arbitrary endpoint data, and when there is one these equations can have more than one pair of constants. These are the possible stationary <catenoids>, so the <axisymmetric second variation of a catenoid> still matters when deciding whether they minimize <surface area>.

= iii
{parent=2007/g/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/g/2/iii}

The functional is unbounded above. Let $\ell$ be the positive affine interpolant of the endpoint values and choose a nonnegative, nonzero, continuously differentiable $h$ vanishing at both endpoints. The admissible functions $y_M=\ell+Mh$ satisfy
$$
J[y_M]\ge2\pi\int_{x_1}^{x_2}y_M\,dx\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
This excludes a global maximum, but **does not prove that a stationary curve is a minimum**. The concluding assertion is false without additional conditions.

An explicit counterexample uses $x_1=-2$, $x_2=2$, $y_1=y_2=\cosh2$ and the stationary function $y=\cosh x$. Its <second variation>, for a perturbation $h$ vanishing at the endpoints, is
$$
\frac{\delta^2J[h]}{2\pi}
=\int_{-2}^{2}\bigl(2\tanh x\,hh'+\operatorname{sech}^2x\,h'^2\bigr)dx
=\int_{-2}^{2}\operatorname{sech}^2x\,(h'^2-h^2)\,dx.
$$
Choose $h=1-x^2/4$. Since $\operatorname{sech}^2x\le4e^{-2|x|}$, the positive contribution is at most $\int_{\mathbb R}x^2e^{-2|x|}dx=1/2$. On $[-1,1]$, $h^2\ge9/16$, so the magnitude of the negative contribution is at least $(9/8)\tanh1>9/16>1/2$. Thus $\delta^2J[h]<0$. Small perturbations in this direction preserve positivity and the endpoint data while decreasing <surface area>. Consequently this <catenoid> is not a local minimum. The valid conclusion from part (ii) is a necessary stationary form; minimization requires a separate stability and existence analysis.

= H
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 2
{parent=2007/h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/h/2}

Write $q=(x,X)^{\mathsf T}$, $M=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\b&c\end{pmatrix}$ and $K=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\\beta&\gamma\end{pmatrix}$. The <Euler-Lagrange equations> give $M\ddot q+Kq=0$. A <normal mode> is a motion $q=v\cos(\omega t+\varphi)$ with fixed nonzero <vector> $v$; its <normal frequency> is $\omega>0$. Substitution gives $(K-\omega^2M)v=0$. Thus the squared <normal frequencies> are the assumed positive distinct roots of
$$
(ac-b^2)\lambda^2-(\alpha c+\gamma a-2\beta b)\lambda+(\alpha\gamma-\beta^2)=0.
$$
For either root, solve the associated two linear equations for the mode ratio.

For the rods, the small angles are $\theta=x/a$ and $\phi=(X-x)/(2a)$. The first rod has <kinetic energy> $m\dot x^2/6$. The second rod's center has horizontal <velocity> $(\dot x+\dot X)/2$ and its <angular velocity> is $(\dot X-\dot x)/(2a)$. Its center-of-mass <moment of inertia> is $2ma^2/3$, giving
$$
T_2=\frac m4(\dot x+\dot X)^2+\frac m{12}(\dot X-\dot x)^2.
$$
Adding these <kinetic energies> gives $T=m(3\dot x^2+2\dot x\dot X+2\dot X^2)/6$. The first center rises by $a\theta^2/4$, and the second by $a\theta^2/2+a\phi^2/2$. Multiplying by the respective masses and $g$ gives
$$
V=\frac{mg}{4a}\bigl[5x^2+(X-x)^2\bigr]
=\frac{mg}{4a}(6x^2-2xX+X^2).
$$
Therefore the stated <Lagrangian> is $T-V$.

Set $\mu=2a\omega^2/(3g)$. The mode equation is
$$
\det\left[\begin{pmatrix}6&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}
-\mu\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\right]
=5\mu^2-17\mu+5=0.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\omega_\pm^2=\frac{3g}{20a}(17\pm3\sqrt{21}).}
$$
The mode ratio is $r=X/x=(6-3\mu)/(1+\mu)$, so the ratio of the rods' angles is $\phi/\theta=(r-1)/2=(5-4\mu)/[2(1+\mu)]$. It is positive for $\mu_-=(17-3\sqrt{21})/10$ and negative for $\mu_+=(17+3\sqrt{21})/10$. Thus the lower-frequency <normal mode> has the rods **in phase**, and the higher-frequency <normal mode> has them **out of phase**.

= J
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 2
{parent=2007/j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/j/2}

In vacuum, the source-free <Maxwell equations> are
$$
\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0,\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\partial_t\mathbf B,\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf B=c^{-2}\partial_t\mathbf E.
$$
Put $\psi=\omega(t-z/c)$ and denote the constant electric amplitude by $E_0$. A corresponding traveling-wave <magnetic field> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf B=\frac{E_0}{c}(-\sin\psi,\cos\psi,0).}
$$
Both <divergences> vanish because both the <electric field> and the <magnetic field> depend only on $z,t$ and have zero $z$ component. Direct differentiation gives $\nabla\times\mathbf E=(\omega E_0/c)(\cos\psi,\sin\psi,0)=-\partial_t\mathbf B$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf B=(\omega E_0/c^2)(-\sin\psi,\cos\psi,0)=c^{-2}\partial_t\mathbf E$. A time-independent background <magnetic field> satisfying the homogeneous equations could be added; the formula is the wave component.

Introduce the <magnetic vector potential> and <electric potential> by $\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$ and $\mathbf E=-\nabla\phi-\partial_t\mathbf A$. These identities automatically give the divergence equation for $\mathbf B$ and <Faraday's law>. For this wave choose $\phi=0$ and $\mathbf A=(c/\omega)\mathbf B$; the <derivatives> just calculated verify both potential equations.

The electromagnetic <force> is the <Lorentz force>, $Q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$. On the smooth constraint plane $z=z_0$, the <constraint force> is normal to the plane. Both $\mathbf v$ and $\mathbf B$ are tangential, so $\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$ is normal and is balanced by that <constraint force>; it does not affect the tangential motion. Thus
$$
\ddot{\mathbf r}_\parallel=\frac{QE_0}{m}(\cos\psi,\sin\psi).
$$
A particular motion is $\mathbf r_\parallel=\mathbf r_0-[QE_0/(m\omega^2)](\cos\psi,\sin\psi)$, a circle of radius
$$
\boxed{\frac{|QE_0|}{m\omega^2}.}
$$
The general planar motion also includes a constant drift <velocity>; the particle moves on a circle when that drift is zero. The <constraint force> can do no work because it is perpendicular to the <velocity>.

= K
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 3
{parent=2007/k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/k/3}

Orient $C$ counterclockwise around the solid body and let $\mathbf n$ point from the solid into the fluid. The <pressure> <force> is $-\int_Cp\mathbf n\,ds$. Since $\mathbf n\,ds=(dy,-dx)$,
$$
F_x-iF_y=-i\oint_Cp\,d\overline z.
$$
For steady <irrotational flow>, <Bernoulli's equation> gives $p=p_0-\rho|w'|^2/2$. The constant term integrates to zero. The boundary is a <streamline>, so $dw=w'\,dz$ is real there, giving $w'\,dz=\overline{w'}\,d\overline z$. Therefore
$$
F_x-iF_y=\frac{i\rho}{2}\oint_C|w'|^2\,d\overline z
=\frac{i\rho}{2}\oint_C(w')^2\,dz,
$$
which is <Blasius force theorem> with the specified orientation.

The prescribed <stream function> is the imaginary part of $w_0(z)=\alpha(z-c)^3$. The zero-circulation solution from the <Milne-Thomson circle theorem> is
$$
w(z)=w_0(z)+\overline{w_0(a^2/\overline z)}
=\alpha\bigl[(z-c)^3+(a^2/z-c)^3\bigr].
$$
On $|z|=a$ the two terms are conjugates, so the boundary <stream function> is constant. The added term has no singularities in the exterior and does not change the prescribed growing far-field part. Additive constants are immaterial.

Differentiating gives
$$
w'=3\alpha\bigl[z^2-2cz+c^2-c^2a^2z^{-2}+2ca^4z^{-3}-a^6z^{-4}\bigr].
$$
The coefficient of $z^{-1}$ in $(w')^2$ is $36\alpha^2ca^2(a^2+c^2)$. The <residue theorem> and <Blasius force theorem> give
$$
\boxed{F_x=-36\pi\rho\alpha^2ca^2(a^2+c^2),\qquad F_y=0.}
$$
The zero-circulation convention is relevant: if an independently imposed <circulation> $\Gamma$ is allowed, adding $\Gamma\log z/(2\pi i)$ changes $F_y$ to $-3\rho\alpha\Gamma c^2$. Thus the claimed zero transverse <force> belongs to the standard circle-theorem construction, or to a single-valued exterior <complex potential of a two-dimensional potential flow>, which excludes that extra circulation.

= 4
{parent=2007/k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/k/4}

For <potential flow>, each <velocity potential> satisfies <Laplace's equation> in its fluid. The exact <kinematic boundary condition> on $y=\eta$ is $\eta_t+\phi_{j,x}\eta_x=\phi_{j,y}$. Keeping only first-order terms gives
$$
\phi_{1,y}=\phi_{2,y}=\eta_t\qquad(y=0).
$$
The linearized <Bernoulli equation> is $p_j=p_0-\rho_j(\phi_{j,t}+g\eta)$ at the interface, so <pressure> <continuity> gives
$$
\boxed{\rho_1\phi_{1,t}-\rho_2\phi_{2,t}+g(\rho_1-\rho_2)\eta=0\qquad(y=0).}
$$
For a decaying disturbance away from the interface, put $\psi=kx-\omega t$ and take
$$
\phi_1=\frac{A\omega}{k}e^{ky}\sin\psi\quad(y<0),\qquad
\phi_2=-\frac{A\omega}{k}e^{-ky}\sin\psi\quad(y>0).
$$
They satisfy <Laplace's equation> and the <kinematic boundary conditions> for $\eta=A\cos\psi$. Substituting in the dynamic condition gives the <dispersion relation>
$$
\boxed{\omega^2=gk\frac{\rho_1-\rho_2}{\rho_1+\rho_2},\qquad
c^2=\frac gk\frac{\rho_1-\rho_2}{\rho_1+\rho_2}.}
$$
If the heavier fluid is on top, $\omega^2<0$. Instead of oscillation, a Fourier disturbance has an exponentially growing component with growth rate $\sqrt{gk(\rho_2-\rho_1)/(\rho_1+\rho_2)}$. This is the <Rayleigh-Taylor instability> in the absence of <surface tension>.

