= a
{parent=2007/m/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/m/5/a}

A <Poisson process> of rate $\lambda$ is a counting process starting at zero, with independent stationary increments, such that an increment over an interval of length $t$ has the <Poisson distribution> of parameter $\lambda t$. It has right-continuous paths with unit jumps almost surely.

Independence of the two processes makes increments of $A+B$ independent on disjoint intervals. The sum of independent <Poisson random variables> with parameters $\alpha t$ and $\beta t$ is <Poisson> with parameter $(\alpha+\beta)t$, as follows by convolution:
$$
\sum_{j=0}^n e^{-\alpha t}\frac{(\alpha t)^j}{j!}
e^{-\beta t}\frac{(\beta t)^{n-j}}{(n-j)!}
=e^{-(\alpha+\beta)t}\frac{((\alpha+\beta)t)^n}{n!}.
$$
Thus $A+B$ is a <Poisson process> of rate $\alpha+\beta$. Its first red and green waiting times are independent <exponential random variables> of rates $\alpha,\beta$, so
$$
P(T_R<T_G)=\int_0^\infty\alpha e^{-\alpha t}e^{-\beta t}\,dt
=\boxed{\frac\alpha{\alpha+\beta}}.
$$

= b
{parent=2007/m/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/m/5/b}

The state-zero <stationary distribution> equation is $\pi_0=q\pi_0+q\pi_1$, so $\pi_1=(p/q)\pi_0$. The state-one equation is $\pi_1=p\pi_0+q\pi_2$, giving $\pi_2=(p/q)\pi_1$. More generally, the stationary <probability> flow across the cut $\{0,\ldots,i\}$ is balanced, so $p\pi_i=q\pi_{i+1}$. This also follows inductively from $\pi_i=p\pi_{i-1}+q\pi_{i+1}$.

Hence $\pi_i=(p/q)^i\pi_0$. Since $p<q$, the <geometric series> is summable, and normalization gives
$$
\boxed{\pi_i=\left(1-\frac pq\right)\left(\frac pq\right)^i,\qquad i\ge0.}
$$
These <probabilities> satisfy <detailed balance> and therefore all the stationarity equations. The <Markov chain> is an <irreducible Markov chain>, so this normalized <stationary distribution> is unique.

= O
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 4
{parent=2007/o}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/o/4}

Up to a parameter-independent multinomial factor, the <likelihood function> is $L=\prod_{i,j}\theta_{ij}^{n_{ij}}$. A <Lagrange multiplier> for $\sum\theta_{ij}=1$ gives $n_{ij}/\theta_{ij}=\lambda$, whence $\lambda=n$ and
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{ij}=n_{ij}/n.}
$$
Since all counts are positive, the <log-likelihood function> is strictly concave on the positive simplex and tends to minus infinity at its boundary, proving this is the unique maximum.

Under <independence of random variables>, $\theta_{ij}=\phi_i\rho_j$, so the <log-likelihood function> separates as $\sum_i n_{i\cdot}\log\phi_i+\sum_jn_{\cdot j}\log\rho_j$. Applying the same constrained maximization twice gives $\widehat\phi_i=n_{i\cdot}/n$, $\widehat\rho_j=n_{\cdot j}/n$, and
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{ij}^{\,0}=n_{i\cdot}n_{\cdot j}/n^2.}
$$
Twice the difference between the unrestricted and null maximized <log-likelihood functions> is
$$
2\log\frac{\sup L}{\sup_{H_0}L}
=2\sum_{i,j}n_{ij}\log\frac{n_{ij}n}{n_{i\cdot}n_{\cdot j}},
$$
the required <likelihood-ratio test statistic>. Under the <null hypothesis>, its asymptotic <chi-squared distribution> has $(r-1)(c-1)$ <statistical degrees of freedom>, the parameter-count difference $(rc-1)-(r+c-2)$. Here that is four. Since $25.192>q_4=18.467$, **reject independence at the 0.1 percent significance level**. For four <statistical degrees of freedom>, the asymptotic upper-tail <probability> is $e^{-25.192/2}(1+25.192/2)\approx4.60\times10^{-5}$. This establishes an association in these data; it is not a causal conclusion.

= 5
{parent=2007/o}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/o/5}

Write $s=e^{-\lambda t}$ for individual survival <probability>. Conditional on a clump of size $n$, its death <probability> is $(1-s)^n$. Averaging with the <geometric distribution> gives
$$
P(\text{dead})=(1-\theta)\sum_{n=1}^\infty\theta^{n-1}(1-s)^n
=\frac{(1-\theta)(1-s)}{1-\theta+\theta s}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{p(t,\lambda)=\frac{s}{1-\theta+\theta s}.}
$$
Here $0\le\theta<1$ and $t>0$; different clumps and experimental repetitions are understood independent for the sampling model.

The live-clump counts have independent <binomial distributions>, all with the same $p$. Up to a constant, their combined <likelihood function> is $p^R(1-p)^{N-R}$, maximized at $\widehat p=R/N$ when $0<R<N$. Solving $p=s/(1-\theta+\theta s)$ for $s$ gives $s=p(1-\theta)/(1-\theta p)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\widehat\lambda=\frac1t\log\frac{N-\theta R}{R(1-\theta)}.}
$$
Monotonicity of $p$ in $\lambda$ makes this the <maximum-likelihood estimator>. At the sampling boundaries, $R=N$ gives $\widehat\lambda=0$, while $R=0$ gives only a supremum as $\lambda\to\infty$, with no finite maximizer. These are the limiting interpretations of the formula.

