= a
{parent=2007/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/3/a}

The printed product omits the node $x_0$: its lower index must be zero for the stated formula to hold. For example, with $n=1$, nodes $0,1$, and data $f_0=0,f_1=1,g_0=g_1=0$, the printed $L_{1,1}=1$ gives $h=1$, which fails the first value condition. Use the corrected <Lagrange basis polynomials>
$$
L_k(x)=\prod_{\substack{0\le i\le n\\i\ne k}}\frac{x-x_i}{x_k-x_i}.
$$
They satisfy $L_k(x_j)=\delta_{kj}$. Define $H_k=L_k^2[1-2(x-x_k)L_k'(x_k)]$ and $K_k=L_k^2(x-x_k)$. At a node with $j\ne k$, the double zero of $L_k^2$ gives $H_k(x_j)=H_k'(x_j)=K_k(x_j)=K_k'(x_j)=0$. At $x_k$, direct differentiation gives
$$
H_k(x_k)=1,\quad H_k'(x_k)=0,\quad K_k(x_k)=0,\quad K_k'(x_k)=1.
$$
Both <polynomials> have degree at most $2n+1$. Consequently $h=\sum_k(f_kH_k+g_kK_k)$ has the specified values and <derivatives>, and is the required <Hermite interpolating polynomial>. The source product is retained literally in the private transcription; this solution identifies its necessary correction.

= b
{parent=2007/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/3/b}

For $d=p-q$, all $d(x_i)$ and $d'(x_i)$ vanish. Between each consecutive pair of nodes, <Rolle's theorem> gives one additional zero of $d'$. Along with the $n+1$ nodal zeros, this supplies $2n+1$ distinct zeros on the closed interval. Since $d'$ is a <polynomial> of degree at most $2n$, it must be the zero <polynomial>. Thus $d$ is constant, and $d(x_0)=0$ makes it zero. Hence **the interpolating <polynomial> is unique**.

The direct zero count includes the two endpoints; its guaranteed interior count before the degree argument is $2n-1$. After $d'=0$ is proved, $d'$ vanishes at every point of the open interval, so the stronger interior-zero assertion in the source follows as well. Another immediate uniqueness proof is that $d$ has a double zero at every node, so is divisible by $\prod_i(x-x_i)^2$ of degree $2n+2$, although its own degree is at most $2n+1$.

= c
{parent=2007/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/3/c}

Use the corrected <Hermite interpolating polynomial> from part (a). The auxiliary function $\varphi$ vanishes at all $n+1$ nodes and at the additional point $x$. Its <derivative> vanishes at all nodes, because both $e'(x_i)$ and the <derivative> of $\pi^2$ vanish there. <Rolle's theorem> between the $n+2$ ordered zeros of $\varphi$ gives $n+1$ further, distinct zeros of $\varphi'$. Thus $\varphi'$ has $2n+2$ distinct zeros on the closed interval. The printed phrase that these include $x$ is not generally true; the extra zeros lie in intervals between zeros of $\varphi$, and $\varphi'(x)$ need not vanish.

Repeated application of <Rolle's theorem> gives $\varphi^{(2n+2)}(\xi)=0$ for some interior $\xi$. Since $h$ has degree at most $2n+1$ and $\pi^2$ is <monic> of degree $2n+2$,
$$
0=f^{(2n+2)}(\xi)-\frac{e(x)}{\pi(x)^2}(2n+2)!.
$$
Rearranging gives
$$
\boxed{e(x)=\frac{f^{(2n+2)}(\xi)}{(2n+2)!}\prod_{i=0}^n(x-x_i)^2.}
$$
At an interpolation node, both sides are zero, so the error formula extends to the whole closed interval. The false location claim about one zero is unnecessary for this result.

= d
{parent=2007/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/3/d}

For nodes $0,1$ and $f(x)=e^x$, the cubic <Hermite interpolating polynomial> is
$$
h(x)=1+x+(2e-5)x^2+(3-e)x^3.
$$
Indeed its values at zero and one are $1,e$, and its <derivatives> there are also $1,e$. The <Hermite interpolation error formula> gives
$$
|e^x-h(x)|=\frac{e^\xi}{4!}x^2(1-x)^2
\le\frac e{24}\left(\frac14\right)^2=\boxed{\frac e{384}},
$$
since $0\le\xi\le1$ and $x(1-x)\le1/4$ on $[0,1]$.

= 4
{parent=2007/p}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2007/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/4/a}

The <Gershgorin circle theorem> states that every complex <eigenvalue> of a square <matrix> $A$ lies in at least one closed disc $D_i=\{z:|z-a_{ii}|\le\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\}$. To prove it, take a nonzero <eigenvector> $v$ for $\lambda$ and choose $i$ with $|v_i|$ maximal. The $i$th row of $Av=\lambda v$ gives
$$
|\lambda-a_{ii}|\,|v_i|
=\left|\sum_{j\ne i}a_{ij}v_j\right|
\le\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\,|v_j|
\le\left(\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\right)|v_i|.
$$
Division by $|v_i|>0$ proves the claim. The proof applies to real and complex <matrices> alike.

= b
{parent=2007/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/4/b}

The second part of the <Gershgorin circle theorem> states that if a union of $k$ of the discs is disjoint from every remaining disc, that union contains exactly $k$ <eigenvalues>, counted with <algebraic multiplicity>. In particular, a single isolated disc contains precisely one <eigenvalue>. Connected components of the full union therefore carry the corresponding disc counts.

= c
{parent=2007/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/4/c}

Write $q=1/4+\epsilon$ and take $0<\epsilon<1/4$, as intended by the small-parameter assumption. The row discs have centers $1,\ldots,n$ and radii $\epsilon,q,\ldots,q,1/4$. They are pairwise disjoint because the sum of neighboring radii is below one. The second <Gershgorin circle theorem> places one <eigenvalue> in each disc. Since $A$ is real, nonreal <eigenvalues> occur in conjugate pairs; a disc centered on the real axis would have to contain both members, contradicting its count of one. Thus all <eigenvalues> are real and
$$
\boxed{|\lambda_1-1|\le\epsilon,\quad
|\lambda_i-i|\le\tfrac14+\epsilon\ (2\le i<n),\quad
|\lambda_n-n|\le\tfrac14.}
$$
Alternatively, a positive diagonal change of <basis> symmetrizes the tridiagonal <matrix>, with transformed off-diagonal entries $\sqrt\epsilon/2$, also proving reality.

= d
{parent=2007/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/4/d}

The <Gershgorin discs> of $A^{\mathsf T}$ have radii $1/4,q,\ldots,q,\epsilon$. They too are disjoint and have one <eigenvalue> each. The disc centered at $n$ therefore improves the final bound to
$$
\boxed{n-\epsilon\le\lambda_n\le n+\epsilon.}
$$
It selects the same <eigenvalue> as before because all other discs lie a fixed positive distance from the disc centered at $n$.

= e
{parent=2007/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/4/e}

Assume $n\ge3$, so the corner entry is separate from the superdiagonal. For $A(\delta)$, the row radii are
$$
r_1=\delta+\epsilon,\qquad r_i=q=\tfrac14+\epsilon\ (2\le i<n),\qquad r_n=\tfrac14.
$$
Thus every <eigenvalue> lies in the union $\bigcup_iD(i,r_i)$. The discs with centers $2,\ldots,n$ remain mutually disjoint. The expanding disc centered at one meets the disc centered at $j$ exactly when $j-1\le\delta+\epsilon+r_j$.

Let $I_\delta=\{1\}\cup\{j\ge2:j-1\le\delta+\epsilon+r_j\}$. Then the union of discs indexed by $I_\delta$ contains exactly $|I_\delta|$ <eigenvalues>; every other disc contains exactly one. Every isolated <eigenvalue> is real by conjugation and obeys $|\lambda-j|\le r_j$. Once discs merge, the second <Gershgorin circle theorem> gives a bound and count for their whole component, rather than one <eigenvalue> per original disc. <Eigenvalue> branches coming from that component stay in its union, but may no longer be distinguished by individual discs. These statements describe the bounds through all merger thresholds as $\delta$ increases.

= f
{parent=2007/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/p/4/f}

The claimed endpoint bounds with radius $\epsilon$ are **false for positive corner perturbations**. For example, take $n=3$, $\epsilon=1/1000$ and $\delta=9/10<1-\epsilon$. The characteristic <polynomial> is
$$
P(\lambda)=(\lambda-1)(\lambda-2)(\lambda-3)
-\frac1{4000}(2\lambda-4)-\frac9{160}.
$$
Throughout $|\lambda-1|\le1/1000$, the absolute value of the sum of its first two terms is below $0.003$, whereas $9/160=0.05625$. Hence this interval contains no root, contradicting the first requested bound. The actual <eigenvalues> are approximately $1.029144,1.943599,3.027257$, which also violate the last requested bound.

There is a valid real-eigenvalue conclusion with wider endpoint bounds. For $0<\epsilon<1/4$ and $\delta<(n-1)/2-\epsilon$, the <Gershgorin discs> from rows have one enlarged component on the left; the <Gershgorin discs> from columns have one enlarged component on the right. Each has an enlarged radius $r=\delta+\epsilon<(n-1)/2$. A small disc that meets the enlarged left disc has index $j$ satisfying $j-1\le r+q<n/2$. A small disc meeting the enlarged right disc satisfies $n-j<n/2$. Thus, when $n=2m$, every disc with index at most $m$ is isolated in the column family and every disc with index at least $m+1$ is isolated in the row family. Their counts give all $n$ <eigenvalues>, one in each disjoint small disc, so all are real.

When $n=2m+1$, the same argument isolates the first $m$ discs in the column family and the last $m$ in the row family. It accounts for $2m$ real <eigenvalues>. The one remaining <eigenvalue> is real by conjugation, and belongs to the central small disc: the enlarged left and right discs are disjoint, and their two components can intersect only within the disc centered at $m+1$. Therefore the valid uniform bounds are
$$
\boxed{|\lambda_1-1|\le\tfrac14,\quad
|\lambda_i-i|\le\tfrac14+\epsilon\ (2\le i<n),\quad
|\lambda_n-n|\le\tfrac14.}
$$
The sharper endpoint radii $\epsilon$ hold in part (d) when $\delta=0$, and cannot simply be carried over to the perturbed <matrix>.

= R
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 2
{parent=2007/r}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/r/2}

Choose a <spanning tree> of the connected plane <graph>. A <tree> with $n$ <vertices> has $n-1$ <edges> and one <face of a plane graph>, so $n-(n-1)+1=2$. Add the remaining drawn <edges> one at a time. Each joins two existing <vertices> and closes a <cycle in a graph>; its interior lies in one current <face of a plane graph>, splitting that <face of a plane graph> into two by the <Jordan curve theorem>. Thus $m$ and $f$ increase together while $n$ stays fixed. The quantity $n-m+f$ remains two, proving <Euler formula for a connected planar graph>.

= i
{parent=2007/r/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/r/2/i}

Every facial boundary <walk> has length at least five. Bridge excursions can repeat an <edge>, but deleting those excursions leaves a boundary containing a <cycle in a graph>; the finite girth assumption ensures the <graph> is not a <tree>. Thus the shortest-cycle condition still gives the lower bound five even when bridges occur. Summing boundary lengths counts each <edge> twice, so $5f\le2m$. Using <Euler formula for a connected planar graph>,
$$
2=n-m+f\le n-m+\frac{2m}{5}=n-\frac{3m}{5}.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{m\le\frac53(n-2).}
$$

= ii
{parent=2007/r/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/r/2/ii}

The <Petersen graph> has ten <vertices>, fifteen <edges>, and girth five. One way to verify the girth is its description by the two-element subsets of a five-element set, with adjacency meaning disjointness. A triangle would require three pairwise disjoint two-element subsets, which is impossible. Two distinct <vertices> have at most one common neighbor: the only possible common neighbor is a two-element subset of the complement of their union. Therefore a four-cycle is impossible. A five-cycle exists in its standard pentagon drawing.

If it were a <planar graph>, part (i) would give $15\le(5/3)(10-2)=40/3$, a contradiction. Thus **the Petersen <graph> is nonplanar**. The held source has blank space where its drawing is mentioned; the named <graph> determines this argument without that missing illustration.

= iii
{parent=2007/r/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/r/2/iii}

For a fixed plane embedding, place one dual <vertex> in each <face of a plane graph> and draw a dual <edge> across each original <edge>, joining its incident faces. These dual <edges> can be drawn without mutual crossings, so this constructs a plane <dual graph>. Each original <vertex> corresponds to a dual <face of a plane graph>, while each <edge> gives exactly one dual <edge>. Hence
$$
\boxed{n^*=f,\qquad m^*=m,\qquad f^*=n.}
$$
Bridges give dual loops and parallel dual <edges> are allowed. On the sphere the dual of the resulting embedding is the original embedding, so planarity is preserved in both directions for this dual pair. The geometric <dual graph> is defined using an embedding; the converse is understood in that setting, rather than as a definition for an arbitrary nonplanar drawing.

= iv
{parent=2007/r/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/r/2/iv}

Take a root $r$ with children $a,b$. Give $a$ the children $c,1$, give $c$ the children $2,3$, and give $b$ the children $4,5$. The <root of a tree> has degree two, the three <vertices> $a,b,c$ have degree three, and $1,\ldots,5$ are the five <leaves of a graph>. There are nine <vertices> and eight <edges>, as required by the <handshaking lemma> and the <edge> count for a <tree>.

\Image[2007-tree-and-dual.png]
{title=A plane <tree> and its one-vertex <dual graph>, with two alternating face colours}

= a
{parent=2007/r/2/iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/r/2/iv/a}

A plane <tree> has one <face of a plane graph>, so its <dual graph> has one <vertex>. Every original <edge> is a bridge incident to that same face on both sides, so every dual <edge> is a loop. Thus the dual drawing in the figure is a bouquet of eight loops, arranged with the displayed nesting inherited from the <tree>. It has nine <faces of a plane graph>, matching $f^*=n=9$. The nesting matters for which faces meet; a bouquet drawn as eight unrelated petals would have a different face-adjacency <graph>.

= b
{parent=2007/r/2/iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/r/2/iv/b}

Two dual <faces of a plane graph> share an <edge> exactly when their corresponding original <vertices> are adjacent. Their face-adjacency <graph> is therefore the original <tree>, which is a nontrivial <bipartite graph>. Colour the face for $r$ with one colour and alternate along the original <tree>. This gives a proper <face colouring> with two colours, and at least two are needed because there is an <edge> separating distinct faces. Hence the minimum is
$$
\boxed{2.}
$$

= S
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 3
{parent=2007/s}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2007/s/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/s/3/a}

Take a shortest allowed simple <graph path>. If it does not use $k$ as an intermediate <vertex>, its length is at least $u_{ij}^{(k-1)}$. If it does, simplicity makes $k$ occur only once. Splitting there gives an $i$--$k$ path and a $k$--$j$ path whose intermediate <vertices> are in $\{1,\ldots,k-1\}$. Their lengths are at least the respective two minima. In either case,
$$
\boxed{u_{ij}^{(k)}\ge\min\{u_{ij}^{(k-1)},u_{ik}^{(k-1)}+u_{kj}^{(k-1)}\}.}
$$
For identical endpoints use the usual empty path of length zero.

= b
{parent=2007/s/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/s/3/b}

An old shortest <graph path> is still allowed at stage $k$, so $u_{ij}^{(k)}\le u_{ij}^{(k-1)}$. Concatenating shortest $i$--$k$ and $k$--$j$ paths gives a <walk> of length $u_{ik}^{(k-1)}+u_{kj}^{(k-1)}$, but it may repeat <vertices>. Every repeated section is a closed <walk>. In the absence of negative-length <cycles in a graph>, each such closed <walk> has nonnegative length: decompose it into simple cycles. Removing these sections therefore produces an allowed simple <graph path> of no greater length. This proves the other upper bound. Together with part (a),
$$
\boxed{u_{ij}^{(k)}=\min\{u_{ij}^{(k-1)},u_{ik}^{(k-1)}+u_{kj}^{(k-1)}\}.}
$$

= c
{parent=2007/s/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/s/3/c}

Initialize an $n\times n$ array $d$ with $d_{ii}=0$ and $d_{ij}=a_{ij}$ for $i\ne j$. For $k=1,\ldots,n$, replace every entry in place by
$$
d_{ij}\leftarrow\min\{d_{ij},d_{ik}+d_{kj}\}.
$$
This is the <Floyd-Warshall algorithm>. At the start of stage $k$, the induction hypothesis is $d_{ij}=u_{ij}^{(k-1)}$. During that stage, row $k$ and column $k$ do not change: $d_{kk}=0$ and no negative cycle can lower it. Hence the operands $d_{ik},d_{kj}$ remain the stage-start values even with in-place updates. Part (b) proves that after the stage every entry is $u_{ij}^{(k)}$. Induction gives all shortest path lengths after stage $n$. The algorithm uses $n^2$ stored lengths and $O(n^3)$ arithmetic operations, with only a fixed number of loop counters beyond the array.

= d
{parent=2007/s/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/s/3/d}

Use four <vertices>, with lengths $a_{12}=a_{24}=0$, $a_{23}=a_{32}=-1$, and every other non-loop arc of length $100$. The two arcs between $2$ and $3$ form a negative-length <cycle in a graph>. For $i=1$, $j=4$, $k=3$,
$$
u_{14}^{(2)}=0,\qquad u_{13}^{(2)}=-1,\qquad u_{34}^{(2)}=-1.
$$
The recurrence would give $-2$, by concatenating $1\to2\to3$ and $3\to2\to4$. That <walk> repeats $2$, so it is not a simple <graph path>. Every simple <graph path> from $1$ to $4$ still has length at least zero, attained by $1\to2\to4$. Thus
$$
\boxed{u_{14}^{(3)}=0>-2,}
$$
a strict inequality in part (a).

= 4
{parent=2007/s}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2007/s/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/s/4/a}

For every <perfect matching> $M$, each <vertex> occurs exactly once, so the feasibility inequalities give
$$
\sum_{e\in M}c_e\ge\sum_{\{u,v\}\in M}(x_u+x_v)=\sum_{v\in V}x_v.
$$
If a <perfect matching> $M^*$ uses only tight <edges>, equality holds. It therefore attains a lower bound valid for every <perfect matching>, proving **minimum cost**. In a <bipartite graph> this is the <weak duality> and <complementary slackness> certificate for the <assignment problem>. The <Hungarian algorithm> maintains feasible row and column potentials and seeks a <perfect matching> among tight <edges>.

= b
{parent=2007/s/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/s/4/b}

Begin with $u=v=0$ and the printed matching $\{(1,1),(3,2),(5,3)\}$. Grow an alternating <tree> from unmatched row two. Its tight <edge> reaches column two, whose matched row is three. Thus the reached row set is $S=\{2,3\}$ and the reached column set is $T=\{2\}$. Among unreached columns the reduced costs are $(6,5,4,4)$ from row two and $(3,4,2,3)$ from row three, so their minimum is two.

Increase $u_2,u_3$ by two and decrease $v_2$ by two. The new tight <edge> $(3,4)$ reaches an unmatched column. Augment along $2\to2\to3\to4$, replacing $(3,2)$ by $(2,2),(3,4)$. The matching becomes $\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,4),(5,3)\}$.

Now grow from unmatched row four. <Edge> $(4,1)$ reaches matched row one, and <edge> $(1,5)$ reaches unmatched column five. Augment along $4\to1\to1\to5$, replacing $(1,1)$ by $(4,1),(1,5)$. This yields
$$
\boxed{\{(1,5),(2,2),(3,4),(4,1),(5,3)\},\qquad\text{cost }2.}
$$
No further potential update is needed. The final potentials are $u=(0,2,2,0,0)$ and $v=(0,-2,0,0,0)$. Their reduced-cost <matrix> is
$$
(c_{ij}-u_i-v_j)=
\begin{pmatrix}0&2&2&1&0\\4&0&3&2&2\\1&0&2&0&1\\0&5&1&2&1\\3&3&0&0&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Every entry is nonnegative and every chosen entry is zero. The dual sum is $\sum u_i+\sum v_j=2$, so part (a) certifies optimality.

= c
{parent=2007/s/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/s/4/c}

Before a potential update, let $S$ be the reached rows and $T$ the reached columns in an alternating forest grown from unmatched rows. Every reached column is matched, or an augmentation would already have occurred, and its matched row is reached. Each <tree> has one more reached row than reached column, so $|S|-|T|>0$.

The update amount is the smallest slack across $S\times T^c$. It is strictly positive because all zero-slack <edges> have already been explored, and the complete <assignment problem> guarantees that the set is nonempty before a <perfect matching> is found. Increasing $u_i$ for $i\in S$ and decreasing $v_j$ for $j\in T$ therefore increases the dual sum by
$$
\boxed{\Delta(|S|-|T|)>0.}
$$
All feasibility inequalities remain valid by the definition of the minimum slack.

= T
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= 2
{parent=2007/t}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2007/t/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/a}

Let $x_j\ge0$ be the production amounts of the three distinct products; the repeated first-product labels in the printed table are interpreted using the surrounding text and the three separate columns. The <linear program> is
$$
\boxed{\max(3x_1+4x_2+8x_3)\quad\text{subject to}\quad
2x_1+3x_2+5x_3\le33,\quad x_1+2x_2+3x_3\le18,\quad x_j\ge0.}
$$

= b
{parent=2007/t/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/b}

Introduce nonnegative <slack variables> $s_1,s_2$ and use a <simplex tableau> with the last row representing $z-c^{\mathsf T}x=0$. The starting tableau is
$$
\begin{array}{c|rrrrr|r}
&x_1&x_2&x_3&s_1&s_2&\text{RHS}\\\hline
s_1&2&3&5&1&0&33\\
s_2&1&2&3&0&1&18\\\hline
z&-3&-4&-8&0&0&0
\end{array}
$$
Enter $x_3$ and let $s_2$ leave. Dividing the second row by three and eliminating its column gives
$$
\begin{array}{c|rrrrr|r}
&x_1&x_2&x_3&s_1&s_2&\text{RHS}\\\hline
s_1&1/3&-1/3&0&1&-5/3&3\\
x_3&1/3&2/3&1&0&1/3&6\\\hline
z&-1/3&4/3&0&0&8/3&48
\end{array}.
$$
Enter $x_1$ and let $s_1$ leave. The next <simplex tableau> is
$$
\begin{array}{c|rrrrr|r}
&x_1&x_2&x_3&s_1&s_2&\text{RHS}\\\hline
x_1&1&-1&0&3&-5&9\\
x_3&0&1&1&-1&2&3\\\hline
z&0&1&0&1&1&51
\end{array}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{(x_1,x_2,x_3)=(9,0,3),\qquad z_{\max}=51.}
$$
The pivot choices and the optimality certificate are justified in the following parts.

= i
{parent=2007/t/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/b/i}

For this tableau sign convention, a negative coefficient in the objective row means the corresponding nonbasic <variable> can increase profit. Initially $x_3$ has the most negative coefficient. The <simplex ratio test> gives $33/5$ in row one and $18/3=6$ in row two, so row two leaves to preserve feasibility. Next $x_1$ is the only column with a negative objective coefficient. The ratios are $3/(1/3)=9$ and $6/(1/3)=18$, so row one leaves. All resulting <basic variables> remain nonnegative.

= ii
{parent=2007/t/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/b/ii}

The last objective row is $z+x_2+s_1+s_2=51$. Every feasible solution has nonnegative $x_2,s_1,s_2$, so $z\le51$. The displayed <basic feasible solution> has all three zero and attains 51, proving optimality. Equivalently, the resource dual prices $(1,1)$ give the same upper bound $33+18=51$ and satisfy <complementary slackness>.

= c
{parent=2007/t/2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2007/t/2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/c/i}

In the usual <linear programming sensitivity within a fixed optimal basis>, retain the optimal <basis> $\{x_1,x_3\}$ while allowing its values to adjust. If the second resource amount is $b$, then
$$
\begin{pmatrix}x_1\\x_3\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}2&5\\1&3\end{pmatrix}^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}33\\b\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}99-5b\\2b-33\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The dual prices remain $(1,1)$, so optimality persists exactly while both <basic variables> are nonnegative:
$$
\boxed{\frac{33}{2}\le b\le\frac{99}{5}.}
$$
The optimal profit on that range is $33+b$. At the endpoints the <basis> is degenerate but remains optimal. If the wording is instead read as requiring the exact numerical production <vector> $(9,0,3)$ to remain unchanged and optimal, only $b=18$ does so; the standard allowable range concerns the <basis> and its adjusted solution.

= ii
{parent=2007/t/2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/c/ii}

Let the third profit coefficient be $c_3$. For the same <basis>, the resource dual prices solve $2y_1+y_2=3$ and $5y_1+3y_2=c_3$, giving
$$
y_1=9-c_3,\qquad y_2=2c_3-15.
$$
Dual feasibility requires $y_1,y_2\ge0$ and $3y_1+2y_2\ge4$. These inequalities are $c_3\le9$, $c_3\ge15/2$ and $c_3\ge7$, respectively. Hence
$$
\boxed{15/2\le c_3\le9.}
$$
The production <vector> is unchanged because the resource constraints are unchanged. Equality at a boundary can allow additional optimal solutions.

= iii
{parent=2007/t/2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/c/iii}

Changing only the second profit coefficient leaves the dual prices at $(1,1)$, because the basic products remain one and three. The nonbasic product has nonpositive <reduced cost> exactly when $c_2\le3(1)+2(1)=5$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{c_2\le5.}
$$
There is no mathematical lower bound; if profit coefficients are additionally restricted to be nonnegative, intersect this range with $[0,\infty)$. At $c_2=5$, alternate optimal solutions are possible.

= d
{parent=2007/t/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/t/2/d}

At the current dual prices $(1,1)$, the new product has <reduced cost> $5-(1+2)=2>0$, so it can increase profit. Its transformed resource column is
$$
B^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}3&-5\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}-7\\3\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Increasing $x_4$ by $t$ changes the old <basic variables> to $x_1=9+7t$ and $x_3=3-3t$. Thus $x_3$ leaves at $t=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)=(16,0,0,1),\qquad z_{\max}=53.}
$$
For a certificate, take dual prices $(1/3,7/3)$. They price the four products at $3,17/3,26/3,5$, respectively, each at least its profit. Their resource total is $33/3+18(7/3)=53$, equal to the achieved profit. <Weak duality> proves optimality, and the profit has increased by two.
